\section{Turn Argument Passing into Context Operations}

An extensional variable denotes a value in an environment. In an intensional target, a name may denote a family of values indexed by contexts, sometimes called worlds. In \emph{The Compilation of a Functional Language into Intensional Logic}, A. A. Yaghi describes compiling a first-order fragment into equations defining nullary symbols. A distinguished result symbol is evaluated in an initial world. The target has no ordinary argument list on its equations; apparent parameters are names whose values depend on the context in which they are demanded. Yaghi contrasts evaluation by rewriting with a demand-driven dataflow architecture for the resulting equations.

One way to implement this model is to number textual calls and use a stack of call-site tags. Entering the callee pushes the tag for that call. To evaluate a formal parameter, consult the tag at the top, select the corresponding actual expression, and pop the tag before evaluating that expression. Popping is essential: the actual belongs to the caller's world. In recursion the same textual tag can be pushed many times; a single register holding the most recent tag would not encode the nested callers. This first-order scheme assumes statically named callees and uniquely resolved formal names.

\section{Worked Example: An Actual in the Caller's World}

Take \(\mathit{inc}(x)=x+1\) and
\(\mathit{result}=\mathit{inc}(\mathit{inc}(3))+\mathit{inc}(7)\).
Tag the outer call on the left \(A\), its nested call \(B\), and the right call \(C\). A zero-argument target sketch is
\begin{verbatim}
result = call[A](inc) + call[C](inc)
inc    = x + 1
x      = select { A: actual[A](call[B](inc));
                  B: actual[B](3); C: actual[C](7) }
\end{verbatim}
Here \texttt{select} inspects the top tag and \texttt{actual} removes it. Starting from the empty context, the left demand enters \texttt{inc} at \([A]\). The demand for \texttt{x} selects the nested call, removes \(A\), and enters \texttt{inc} again at \([B]\); there \texttt{x} resolves to \(3\). The left result is \(5\). The right demand enters at \([C]\), returning \(8\), so the sum is \(13\). A cache keyed only by symbol could return the left result for the right call, or mistake the nested demand for a cycle. Within an execution with fixed external inputs, cache hits require the same symbol and context, unless a separately justified equivalence shows that different contexts yield the same value.

\section{Schedule Demands and Bound Retention}

A demand machine creates work for a pair of name and context. A strict binary primitive requests both operands and waits for both results; a conditional requests its predicate and then only the chosen arm. With a pure semantics, independent operand demands can be scheduled in either order, or concurrently, without changing a defined integer result. Non-strict control is still essential: requesting and waiting for an infinite unused branch can destroy termination. One can test this property by putting a diverging expression in the unchosen arm and instrumenting the scheduler to confirm that it never creates a demand for that branch.

In a parallel evaluator, an in-flight demand can record its subscribers. When the result arrives, it supplies the registered operand port of each waiting computation. Ports must distinguish left and right even when both operands demand the same key, and keys must distinguish contexts even when the names coincide. Replace addition in the worked example with subtraction and deliver the right result first. The answer must still be \(5-8=-3\), not the subtraction in arrival order.

A. Charalambidis, A. Grivas, N. Papaspyrou, and P. Rondogiannis's \emph{Efficient Intensional Implementation for Lazy Functional Languages} describes eliminating higher-order parameters for a class of programs by repeated transformations. Its tag has separate dimensions for successive orders. It also presents lazy activation records whose entries fill on demand, giving a way to avoid expensive general-purpose context lookup in appropriate cases. These are runtime implementation choices, not universal bounds: retaining all demanded name-context pairs can keep whole chains of caller information alive. Profile the number of distinct pairs, cache hit rate, and reachable context size before choosing what to retain. \emph{The Modern Compiler Dossier}, Chapter~8, also uses demand to schedule work, but its delayed work is \emph{compilation}. Intensional demands in this example compute values from equations that have already been compiled.

