\section{Intern Equal Worlds Before Memoizing Demands}

Two independent requests may build identical call histories by pushing the same sequence of tags onto the empty context. Structural equality on linked histories can make a cache hit as expensive as walking the stack. In an illustrative first-order evaluator, intern each nonempty world by the pair consisting of its top tag and the identifier of its tail. The input to a push is a valid site tag and a world identifier created by this interner. Begin with identifier zero for the empty world. In one execution, the postcondition is that two identifiers are equal exactly when their full tag histories are equal.

\begin{verbatim}
push(tag, tail):
  key = (tag, tail)
  if key in intern: return intern[key]
  # Publish only one identifier for each pair.
  id = fresh_id()
  worlds[id] = key; intern[key] = id
  return id
pop(id):
  require id != 0
  return worlds[id].tail
demand_key(symbol, world):
  # Equal top tags alone do not identify equal worlds.
  return (symbol, world)
\end{verbatim}

Push \(A\) onto \(0\) to get identifier \(1\), then \(B\) onto \(1\) to get \(2\). Repeating both operations yields \(1,2\) again; pushing \(B\) directly onto \(0\) gives \(3\), and popping \(2\) gives \(1\). Suppose a pure test symbol counts the number of tags in its world. Demanding it twice at \(2\) gives \(2\) on both requests with one completed-cache entry; demanding it at \(3\) gives \(1\) and creates a second entry. A cache keyed by the top tag would wrongly report \(2\) in the last request.

The invariant follows by induction on history length: every pair has a canonical tail and there is one identifier for each pair. With hashing, pushes and key comparisons then have expected constant cost independent of depth. Concurrent insertion must make lookup and publication atomic with respect to other pushes. An interner holding all worlds forever bounds neither space nor lifetime; reclamation can use weak entries or sweep unused worlds, but identifiers must not be reused while still named by memo entries or active tasks. A simple per-execution interner can instead drop the entire table when that execution ends.
